From 0ded9d5b3bf8c9b904875f0fb98afba4c1d9cec7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
Date: Wed, 3 Jun 2026 22:04:46 +0100
Subject: [PATCH v4 1/2] rust: crypto: add AES-128, AES-CMAC, SHA-256, and HMAC
 bindings

Expose the synchronous lib/crypto AES-128, AES-CMAC, SHA-256, and
HMAC-SHA256 primitives through safe Rust APIs.

Aes128 prepares the key schedule once in Aes128::new and reuses it
across encrypt_block calls, so a caller building an AES-CTR keystream
does not re-expand the key per block. aes_cmac is a separate one-shot
over the in-tree AES-CMAC library and prepares its own key. The hash and
HMAC helpers operate on slices, and every API uses fixed-size outputs.

zeroize wraps memzero_explicit, which has no equivalent in the Rust
standard library, and is used wherever key material is dropped. C shims
cover interfaces that bindgen cannot represent and clear temporary key
material before returning.

RUST_CRYPTO_LIB_AES and RUST_CRYPTO_LIB_SHA256 gate the bindings and
select the C libraries they call. Both are built into the kernel because
Rust abstractions are part of the built-in kernel crate.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
---
